y is implementing pkg/sys/ first, then cmd/version, but I need to stay within the constraint of at most 4 PRD sub-requirements.\n\nThe challenge is I don't have the actual PRD content to reference specific requirement IDs, but the constraint requires anchoring to them. File metadata is clearly the most foundational since every file-operating utility needs it, so I should draft a task around that—but I'm stuck without knowing the exact requirement IDs from prd002-sys. I shouldn't invent requirement IDs if I don't know them—I need to reference actual PRD content instead. My best approach is to title the task with the PRD reference, include the actual PRD file in the required reading, describe what needs building in the task description while pointing to the PRD for specifics, and let the stitch agent read the PRD and implement from there. Looking at the naming pattern, prd002-sys.yaml should exist alongside prd001-testutils and prd003-format, so I'll reference that. Now I need to scope a subset of pkg/sys that's 150-250 lines and covers at most 4 PRD subsections.\n\nFile metadata is the most critical capability for early consumers like ls and du, and terminal size is also essential for column output in ls. Since I can only propose one task, I should focus on what unblocks the most value—pkg/sys is the missing piece for completing rel00.0 since format and testutils already have implementations, so I'll target file metadata as the core deliverable.\n\nThe implementation would abstract platform differences around stat fields like st_mtimespec versus st_mtim and st_blocks handling, with a FileInfo interface and separate implementations for Darwin and Linux—roughly 150-200 lines across a couple of files. This is a coherent unit that's already listed in the architecture spec, so the PRD likely has specific requirements for it. Since implementation and tests should be split into separate tasks, this proposal should focus on the implementation only, leaving tests for a follow-up. I need to anchor this to actual PRD requirement IDs rather than guessing at sub-requirement numbers. Since the stitch agent will read the actual PRD and map requirements, I should reference the PRD structure we know exists while being honest about what I can infer from the architecture and existing code patterns. Let me examine what requirement IDs are already documented in the codebase to establish the right naming convention. I'm noticing a discrepancy—the code_status marks uc002-format and uc004-testutils as \"not started,\" but both packages actually have Go files in the source code, which means they already exist. This tells me the code_status is tracking the roadmap, not the actual repository state. Since pkg/sys/ doesn't appear in the source code at all, that's the package I need to create, and the implementation_status suggests its spec is already complete.\n\nFor the structure, I'll use Go's platform-specific file naming conventions with separate Darwin and Linux implementations, keeping the scope tight to fit within the 4 sub-requirements and line limit.\n\nLooking at the capability groups listed—terminal queries, signal handling, file metadata, process management, and priority—file metadata is the most essential to implement first. I'll focus the task around that single capability group rather than trying to cover all five.\n\nFor the task structure, I'm planning to create a cross-platform file metadata abstraction with separate implementations for Darwin and Linux to handle their different stat field layouts. The requirements will map to specific platform divergences like modification time representation and block count differences, and I'll reference the architecture documents so the stitch agent can validate against the actual PRD. Wrap stat and lstat with platform-independent return types. I need to implement a FileMetadata type that wraps os.FileInfo and handles platform-specific stat fields, then create Stat and Lstat functions that return this