ng to finalize this draft now. Actually, let me reconsider whether pkg/sys is the right priority—looking at the release roadmap, it's the only incomplete foundational piece in rel00.0 and it's blocking several downstream tasks like du and ls, so yes, that's definitely the right choice.\n\nWithin pkg/sys, file metadata is the most critical capability since it's needed across multiple utilities, while terminal size is mainly for ls and other features like signal handling come later. So proposing pkg/sys file metadata makes the most sense.\n\nLet me validate the YAML and wrap this up. One thing I'm noticing though—the existing format code is already doing terminal detection directly with raw syscalls, which suggests there might be some overlap or duplication to consider with how pkg/sys should be structured.\n\nLooking at the scope, file metadata would be around 150-200 lines of implementation versus terminal size at roughly 50 lines, but since file metadata is needed by utilities that don't have implementations yet, that's still the right priority.\n\nI'm considering whether I could combine both into a single task—that would give me roughly 150-200 lines total and stay within the 4 sub-requirement budget by taking 2 from the terminal size group and 2 from file metadata. But mixing capabilities across different requirement groups violates the constraint about not crossing requirement boundaries, so I should stick with file metadata alone.\n\nFor the file metadata task, I need to pick the four most critical sub-requirements: the stat and lstat functions, the modification time abstraction, and the block count abstraction. That leaves room for device ID and inode handling as a follow-up. Now I'm ready to write the final output, making sure to include the requirement ID in the title. I'm laying out the specific files to create—a platform-independent FileMetadata wrapper and Darwin/Linux-specific implementations for extracting stat fields—along with the core requirements that define what the abstraction needs to expose. I'm deciding to use golang.org/x/sys/unix as the only external dependency for accessing platform-specific Stat_t fields, and I'll organize platform-specific code using Go's build constraint file naming convention rather than build tags. The cmd/ packages will import from pkg/sys/ rather than directly from golang.org/x/sys to maintain clean architecture boundaries. I need to ensure the FileMetadata struct and Stat/Lstat functions are properly exported, and that the Darwin and Linux implementations correctly access their respective time and block count fields. I've verified this implementation meets all the constraints—it's a single, focused task with three files that stay well under the line limits, anchored to the PRD requirement, and contains only implementation code without tests. I'm ready to provide the final response.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27938,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27938},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e03769b6-bac6-45a1-aa5a-dd39c17a8b97","uuid":"6da93d22-4c9c-4675-b54e-e03f3d8e19dd"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014Bffc1jvmzYfVZYtWEorUR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `cmd/` (utility implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, scaffolded), `docs/` (human-authored specs).\n\n**Current state**:\n- `pkg/testutils/` — code exists (difftest.go, difftest_test.go in source_code)\n- `pkg/format/` — code exists (color.go, column.go, size.go in source_code)\n- `pkg/sys/` — **no code on working branch** (spec complete, code only at generation-merged tags)\n- `cmd/` utilities — no code on working branch\n\n**Current release**: rel00.0 (Batch 0 — foundational infrastructure). Two of four use cases have code (format, testutils). `pkg/sys/` is the remaining foundational package blocking downstream utilities (ls needs terminal size + file metadata, du needs block counts, cat/sponge need file metadata).\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nrel00.0 is the earliest incomplete release. Within it:\n- uc002-format: code exists ✓\n- uc004-testutils: code exists ✓\n- uc001-magefiles: scaffolded (DD7 excludes from generation; only cmd/version is a target)\n- **uc003-sys: no code — this is the blocker**\n\n`pkg/sys/` file metadata is the most widely needed capability (du needs st_blocks, ls needs stat fields, future utilities need modification times). It should be built before terminal size or signal handling.\n\n## Proposed Tasks\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/sys file metadata abstraction (prd002-sys R3)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml (file metadata requirements -- likely the R3.x group)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys/ component description, design decisions DD3 and DD6, technology_choices for golang.org/x/sys)\n      - go.mod (module path and Go version)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo.go\n        action: create\n        note: Platform-independent FileMetadata type, Stat and Lstat functions\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo_darwin.go\n        action: create\n        note: Darwin-specific stat field extraction (Mtimespec, Blocks)\n      - path: pkg/sys/fileinfo_linux.go\n        action: create\n        note: Linux-specific stat field extraction (Mtim, Blocks)\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement a FileMetadata struct that wraps os.FileInfo and exposes platform-independent access to modification time and disk block count, per the file metadata requirements in prd002-sys\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement Stat(path string) and Lstat(path string) functions that return (*FileMetadata, error), performing the underlying syscall and populating platform-specific fields, per prd002-sys\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"In fileinfo_darwin.go, extract modification time from Stat_t.Mtimespec and block count from Stat_t.Blocks using golang.org/x/sys/unix, per prd002-sys\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"In fileinfo_linux.go, extract modification time from Stat_t.Mtim and block count from Stat_t.Blocks using golang.org/x/sys/unix, per prd002-sys\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use golang.org/x/sys/unix for Stat_t field access -- this is the only approved external dependency per architecture technology_choices\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go build constraint file suffixes (_darwin.go, _linux.go) for platform-specific implementations\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"cmd/ packages import pkg/sys/ for file metadata; they must not import golang.org/x/sys directly, per architecture component boundaries\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"pkg/sys/fileinfo.go exports FileMetadata struct with ModTime() and Blocks() accessors, plus Stat and Lstat function signatures\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"pkg/sys/fileinfo_darwin.go compiles on darwin and correctly reads Mtimespec and Blocks from syscall.Stat_t via golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"pkg/sys/fileinfo_linux.go compiles on linux and correctly reads Mtim and Blocks from syscall.Stat_t via golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go build ./pkg/sys/ succeeds on the current platform with no errors\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/sys/ reports no issues\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":27938,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":27938},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e03769b6-bac6-45a1-aa5a-dd39c17a8b97","uuid":"5d8ebf4f-a649-4ae7-8ecd-dcd2dc0cf4e6"}
